# Some C++ headers and sources in this repo are stored with CRLF line endings
# (a historical accident, but converting them now would rewrite whole files for
# no benefit).  Without this, `git diff --check` reports the CR at end-of-line as
# "trailing whitespace" on every added line in those files, which buries any real
# whitespace problem in the noise.
*.h  whitespace=cr-at-eol
*.hpp whitespace=cr-at-eol
*.cpp whitespace=cr-at-eol
*.cs  whitespace=cr-at-eol

# ── Unity YAML ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Scenes, prefabs and .asset files are YAML that git's line-based merge will
# silently corrupt: it can produce a file that parses but describes a different
# hierarchy, which is worse than a conflict.  UnityYAMLMerge understands them and
# ships inside every editor install, at
#   <editor>/Editor/Data/Tools/UnityYAMLMerge
#
# THESE LINES DO NOTHING ON THEIR OWN.  A merge driver named here has to exist in
# each developer's git config, once per machine:
#
#   git config --global merge.unityyamlmerge.name "Unity YAML merge"
#   git config --global merge.unityyamlmerge.driver \
#     '<editor>/Editor/Data/Tools/UnityYAMLMerge merge -p %O %A %B %A'
#
# The package ships no scenes or prefabs today, so this is a guard placed before
# it is needed rather than after: a demo scene under Samples~ is the obvious
# thing to add next, and adding it is not the moment anyone will remember this.
*.unity   merge=unityyamlmerge  eol=lf
*.prefab  merge=unityyamlmerge  eol=lf
*.asset   merge=unityyamlmerge  eol=lf
*.mat     merge=unityyamlmerge  eol=lf
*.anim    merge=unityyamlmerge  eol=lf
*.controller merge=unityyamlmerge eol=lf

# .meta files are YAML too, but UnityYAMLMerge is documented for scenes and
# prefabs and has nothing useful to say about them.  What matters for a .meta is
# that its guid survives: every scene and prefab in every consuming project
# refers to assets by that guid, so losing or duplicating one silently breaks
# references everywhere, with no error at import time.
#
# They are near-identical to one another apart from that guid, which makes git's
# rename detection pair them up across unrelated files -- a real observation from
# the 1.5.17 recorder move, not a hypothetical.  Left as normal text so conflicts
# surface rather than being auto-resolved; scripts/check-unity-package.sh asserts
# the guids are present, well-formed and unique.
*.meta    eol=lf
